  "worked_solution": "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4x-7 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x & =7 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2\\cdot x & =7 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2\\cdot x+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2} & =7 \\\\ (x+2)^{2}-4 & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}-4 & =7 \\\\ (x+2)^{2} & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $p=2,$ $q=11,$ and $p+q=13.$",
